# Copy to `.env` for local `wrangler dev`. In production set each as a secret:
# `wrangler secret put <NAME>`, or `wrangler deploy --secrets-file .env`.
#
# Per environment, wrangler loads `.env`, then `.env.local`, then — when you
# pass `--env <name>` — `.env.<name>` and `.env.<name>.local`, each overriding
# the last. So a staging deployment is `.env.staging`, not an edit to
# wrangler.jsonc. Everything but this example file is gitignored.
#
# One caveat: if a `.dev.vars` exists it wins outright and none of the above is
# read. This project uses `.env`; keep a single file so there is no question
# which one is live.
#
# Cloudflare API credentials for `npm run cf` are deliberately not here — they
# authenticate you, not the Worker. See `.cf.env.example`.

# One Ed25519 private JWK (a single line) signing every card this Worker serves
# and every push-notification callback JWT. MUST include a `kid`. Generate with
# `npm run keygen`.
#
# Only the matching PUBLIC key is ever exposed, at /.well-known/jwks.json — the
# private half never leaves the Worker.
#
# One key, not one per agent: the card lives at a well-known URI, which RFC 8615
# defines per-authority, so this origin serves one card and the gateway pins one
# key. The agents share this Worker and this file anyway, so per-agent keys never
# expressed a boundary that existed. What separates them is the tenant claim in
# the gateway's token, which core checks against the tenant each request names.
A2A_SIGNING_KEY=

# GATEWAY_ORIGINS: JSON array of trusted gateway origins, e.g.
# ["https://gateway.example.com"]. A property of this deployment, like the key.
# Not a traditional secret, but kept out of wrangler.jsonc so it can differ per
# environment with no code change.
GATEWAY_ORIGINS=

# ARC_API_KEY: API key for the ARC-AGI-3 game API (three.arcprize.org), issued
# from the ARC-AGI-3 web console. Required only by the arc-player agent
# (@loopingai/plugins/arc-agi declares it). Delete this line if you remove it.
ARC_API_KEY=

# There is NO model credential in this file, deliberately — and there is nothing
# to add.
#
# Every agent in this Worker, the coder included, reaches Workers AI through the
# `AI` binding, which Cloudflare authenticates for the Worker. There is no key to
# store, no key to rotate, and the coder's container has never seen one.
#
# Two entries used to live here and were removed on 2026-08-20, when the coder
# moved off Claude:
#
#   ANTHROPIC_PROXY_ORIGIN  named a sibling Worker that held the Anthropic
#                           credential, reached through an AI Gateway custom
#                           provider. That Worker is retired.
#   AI_GATEWAY_TOKEN        authenticated this Worker *to* AI Gateway, needed
#                           only because that path called a gateway URL directly
#                           instead of going through the binding.
#
# If you are configuring a fresh deployment: you need neither. Should a gateway
# `401` ever appear, it means Authenticated Gateway is switched on for the
# gateway named by `aiGatewayId` in src/config.ts — switch it off, because the
# `AI` binding does not send a gateway token.

# GITHUB_TOKEN: used by @loopingai/plugins/repo to clone, push a work branch and
# open the pull request. Needs `contents: write` and `pull-requests: write` on
# the repositories the coder is pointed at — a fine-grained PAT scoped to those
# repos is the right shape, not a classic token with org-wide access.
#
# It never enters the container as a command argument, it is offered only to the
# hosts on the repo plugin's allowlist (github.com by default) — a clone URL
# pointing anywhere else is refused before git runs — and pull requests are
# opened from the Worker rather than the sandbox, so the container never holds a
# credential that can write to the repository through the API.
#
# Required only by the coder agent. Delete this line if you remove it.
GITHUB_TOKEN=

# There is deliberately nothing else here.
#
# `ENVIRONMENT` used to live at the bottom of this file, selecting between
# @cloudflare/sandbox's two backup paths (presigned URLs in production, a direct
# R2 binding under `wrangler dev`). The coder no longer snapshots its container
# to R2 at all — that path required R2 S3-API credentials a Workers binding
# cannot supply, and failed on every task in production — so the var had no
# reader and was removed along with it. See wrangler.jsonc.
